{"action": "diagnose", "why": "The results back the investigator's chain: ledger-consumer was recreated with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, a duplicate webhook for evt_000272 arrived, and the copy handled after the switch was booked again without any dedup step.", "diagnosis": {"hypothesis": "At 20:26:37Z ledger-consumer was recreated with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, a handler that books each Kafka message without checking processed_events. The provider then delivered evt_000272 (payment.succeeded, pay_000247, $104.44) twice. webhook-receiver accepted both and queued them at provider-events/1 offsets 122 and 123, which is normal under at-least-once delivery. Offset 122 went through the dedup step: an INSERT into processed_events ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, then the booking. Offset 123 was handled about 212ms after it was queued. That handler made no processed_events or other dedup query: it inserted a transaction and its entries directly. The ledger now holds two transactions for one event (272 and 275), so pay_000247 shows $208.88 against the provider's $104.44. That is the +$104.44 matches_provider gap and the posted_exactly_once finding. The ledger still balances because both postings were double-entry.", "evidence": [{"call_id": "c1", "shows": "Settled check with nothing left in flight. evt_000272 posted 2 times (transactions 272 and 275). Ledger is +$104.44 over the provider, all on pay_000247 (20888 vs 10444 cents). Ledger balanced. Payments (324) and refunds (31) match the provider, so only the ledger is wrong."}, {"call_id": "c4", "shows": "ledger-consumer was recreated at 20:26:37Z with LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, the only change since 20:25:10Z, when all other services were created."}, {"call_id": "c3", "shows": "webhook-receiver queued evt_000272 twice, at partition 1 offsets 122 (20:26:37.925Z) and 123 (20:26:38.423Z), under different trace ids. These were two webhook deliveries, not a Kafka re-read."}, {"call_id": "c2", "shows": "ledger-consumer logged 'posted' for evt_000272 from both 1@122 (20:26:37.928Z) and 1@123 (20:26:38.635Z)."}, {"call_id": "c6", "shows": "For offset 122, ledger-consumer ran INSERT INTO processed_events ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING before inserting the transaction and its entries. The receiver had returned 200 within 3.9ms."}, {"call_id": "c7", "shows": "For offset 123, ledger-consumer started about 212ms after the send and ran no processed_events query. It inserted into transactions and entries directly, with outcome 'posted'."}, {"call_id": "c5", "shows": "payment-confirmer answered 'already confirmed' for both copies. Its UPDATE is guarded on status='pending', so it made no duplicate change."}], "confidence": 0.85, "proposed_fix": {"kind": "rollback", "target": "ledger-consumer", "summary": "Roll ledger-consumer back to the settings it ran before the 20:26:37Z deploy, the handler that records and checks processed_events, so redelivered events stop being booked twice. The rollback does not repair the books. As a separate, human-reviewed data repair, remove or reverse the duplicate posting for evt_000272: transaction 275 and its entries, keeping 272. Then rerun the money check. Before the repair, confirm that processed_events holds evt_000272. Also confirm that no other event was booked twice after 20:26:37Z (c1 found only one)."}, "blast_radius": "Recreating ledger-consumer pauses ledger booking while it restarts. Kafka keeps the messages and the consumer resumes from its committed offset, so nothing should be lost. The restored handler dedups, so any message read again is skipped, not booked twice. payment-confirmer, webhook-receiver and payments-api are untouched. The data repair writes to the ledger. If the wrong transaction were removed, the books would fall out of balance or out of step with the provider, so it needs a human check of transactions 272 and 275.", "rollback_plan": "If the rolled-back handler misbehaves, for example by stalling or rejecting events, redeploy ledger-consumer with its current settings (LEDGER_HANDLER=naive, BAD_EVENTS=dead_letter) while the cause is found. Watch for duplicates if you do: naive does not dedup. For the data repair, keep a copy of the transaction 275 row and its entries first, so they can be put back if needed.", "fix_action": {"name": "rollback_deploy", "arguments": {"service": "ledger-consumer"}}}}
```
